\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid12334383\charrsid12334383 \hich\af31506\dbch\af31505\loch\f31506 Every month, lenders make thousands of decisions with one unanswered question at their core: which customers will miss their next payment?
\par \hich\af31506\dbch\af31505\loch\f31506 
Get it wrong in one direction, and losses build quietly. Get it wrong in the other, and good customers are turned away. I set out to build a system that answers this question with evidence, and then explains its answer to anyone who has to act on it.

\par \hich\af31506\dbch\af31505\loch\f31506 The data\line \hich\af31506\dbch\af31505\loch\f31506 
The project is based on more than 30,000 real credit card customers of a bank in Taiwan, covering April to September 2005 and published by the UCI Machine Learning Repository. For each customer, the data captures their credit limit, selected personal deta
\hich\af31506\dbch\af31505\loch\f31506 ils, six months of repayment history, bill amounts and payments, and whether they missed the following month\hich\f31506 \rquote \loch\f31506 s payment.
\par \hich\af31506\dbch\af31505\loch\f31506 The approach\line \hich\af31506\dbch\af31505\loch\f31506 
I built a predictive model using logistic regression, a machine learning method valued in credit risk for being both reliable and transparent. The model learns a weight for every feature from historical outcomes: how strongly a late payment, a low credit 
\hich\af31506\dbch\af31505\loch\f31506 limit or a high bill pushes a customer\hich\f31506 \rquote \loch\f31506 
s risk up or down. It combines these weights into a single probability of default for each customer, so every prediction can be traced back to the factors behind it.
