\par \hich\af31506\dbch\af31505\loch\f31506 The evidence\line \hich\af31506\dbch\af31505\loch\f31506 
Tested on customers the model had never seen, it achieved a ROC-AUC of 0.76. Its probabilities were well calibrated: an average estimate of 22.3% against an actual default rate of 22.1%. And its risk groups held up in reality: customers rated high risk, w
\hich\af31506\dbch\af31505\loch\f31506 ith an average estimate of 70%, defaulted at a rate of 68%.
\par \hich\af31506\dbch\af31505\loch\f31506 What the data revealed\line The strongest single signal was recent behaviour. Customers two or more months behind in the latest month defaulted 69% of the time, compared with 17% for everyone else.
\par \hich\af31506\dbch\af31505\loch\f31506 Accuracy alone would have told the wrong story. The model reached 82% accuracy, but simply predicting that no one defaults already scores 78%. Recall, precision and calibration are what show a model\hich\f31506 
\rquote \loch\f31506 s real value.
\par \hich\af31506\dbch\af31505\loch\f31506 
And choosing the warning threshold turned out to be a business decision, not a technical one. At a 50% cut-off, the model identifies about one third of future defaulters, with two thirds of its alerts correct. Lowering the threshold catches more defaulter
\hich\af31506\dbch\af31505\loch\f31506 s but raises more false alarms, so I built an interactive control that lets decision-makers weigh that trade-off themselves.
\par \hich\af31506\dbch\af31505\loch\f31506 Why it matters\line \hich\af31506\dbch\af31505\loch\f31506 \hich\f31506 A risk score is only valuable if people trust it and can act on it. That is why every estimate is phrased in plain terms, for example \'93
\loch\f31506 \hich\f31506 about 66 out of 100 similar customers missed a payment,\'94\loch\f31506  alongside the factors driving it. Responsible use matters too: \hich\af31506\dbch\af31505\loch\f31506 
the dataset includes gender, age and marital status, so any real lending application would require fairness testing and legal review before using such inputs.
\par \hich\af31506\dbch\af31505\loch\f31506 Tools: Python, pandas, scikit-learn, Plotly, REST/JSON API, HTML/CSS/JavaScript, pytest (50 automated tests).
\par \hich\af31506\dbch\af31505\loch\f31506 I would value the perspective of professionals in credit risk and analytics, especially on how you balance predictive power with explainability when models reach real decision-makers.
\par \hich\af31506\dbch\af31505\loch\f31506 Data source: UCI Machine Learning Repository (Yeh & Lien, 2009).
